<h2>Introduction to Medication Administration</h2>


<div class="card">


<p>

Medication administration is the process of safely preparing, giving, and documenting medications for a patient.

</p>


<p>

Medical assistants must understand medication safety, proper techniques, and legal responsibilities when assisting with medications.

</p>


<h3>Goals of Safe Medication Administration</h3>


<ul>

<li>Give the correct medication.</li>

<li>Prevent medication errors.</li>

<li>Protect patient safety.</li>

<li>Educate patients about their medications.</li>

<li>Document accurately.</li>

</ul>


</div>







<h2>Medication Orders</h2>


<div class="card">


<p>

A medication order is a written or electronic instruction from an authorized healthcare provider that allows medication to be administered.

</p>



<h3>Parts of a Medication Order</h3>


<ul>


<li><strong>Patient name:</strong> Identifies who receives the medication.</li>


<li><strong>Date and time:</strong> Shows when the order was written.</li>


<li><strong>Medication name:</strong> Identifies the drug.</li>


<li><strong>Dose:</strong> Amount of medication to administer.</li>


<li><strong>Route:</strong> How the medication enters the body.</li>


<li><strong>Frequency:</strong> How often medication is given.</li>


<li><strong>Provider signature:</strong> Authorizes the order.</li>


</ul>


</div>







<h2>Medication Labels</h2>


<div class="card">


<p>

Before administering medication, always compare the medication label with the provider's order.

</p>


<h3>Check the Label Three Times</h3>


<ol>


<li>When removing medication from storage.</li>


<li>When preparing the medication.</li>


<li>Before returning or administering the medication.</li>


</ol>



<h3>Check the Label For:</h3>


<ul>


<li>Medication name</li>

<li>Strength</li>

<li>Dose</li>

<li>Expiration date</li>

<li>Route</li>

<li>Patient instructions</li>


</ul>


</div>







<h2>Forms of Medication</h2>


<div class="card">


<table>


<tr>

<th>Form</th>

<th>Description</th>

</tr>



<tr>

<td>Tablet</td>

<td>Solid medication taken orally.</td>

</tr>



<tr>

<td>Capsule</td>

<td>Medication enclosed in a dissolvable shell.</td>

</tr>



<tr>

<td>Solution</td>

<td>Medication completely dissolved in liquid.</td>

</tr>



<tr>

<td>Suspension</td>

<td>Medication particles mixed in liquid and must usually be shaken.</td>

</tr>



<tr>

<td>Cream/Ointment</td>

<td>Medication applied to the skin.</td>

</tr>



<tr>

<td>Patch</td>

<td>Medication absorbed through the skin.</td>

</tr>



<tr>

<td>Suppository</td>

<td>Medication inserted into a body cavity.</td>

</tr>



<tr>

<td>Inhaler</td>

<td>Medication delivered through the respiratory system.</td>

</tr>


</table>


</div>

<h2>✅ Rights of Medication Administration</h2>


<div class="card">


<p>

The rights of medication administration help healthcare professionals prevent medication errors and protect patients.

</p>



<h3>The Six Rights</h3>


<ul>


<li>
<strong>Right Patient:</strong>
Verify the patient's identity using approved identifiers.
</li>


<li>
<strong>Right Medication:</strong>
Confirm the medication name matches the provider's order.
</li>


<li>
<strong>Right Dose:</strong>
Verify the correct amount of medication.
</li>


<li>
<strong>Right Route:</strong>
Administer the medication using the correct method.
</li>


<li>
<strong>Right Time:</strong>
Give the medication at the correct scheduled time.
</li>


<li>
<strong>Right Documentation:</strong>
Record the medication administration accurately.
</li>


</ul>



<h3>Additional Rights Often Included</h3>


<ul>


<li>
<strong>Right Reason:</strong>
Know why the patient is receiving the medication.
</li>


<li>
<strong>Right Response:</strong>
Monitor the patient's reaction and effectiveness.
</li>


<li>
<strong>Right to Refuse:</strong>
Patients have the right to refuse medication.
</li>


</ul>


</div>







<h2>Medication Administration Safety Checks</h2>


<div class="card">


<h3>Before Giving Medication</h3>


<ul>


<li>Review the provider's order.</li>

<li>Identify the patient correctly.</li>

<li>Check for allergies.</li>

<li>Verify the medication expiration date.</li>

<li>Check the medication label.</li>

<li>Explain the medication to the patient.</li>


</ul>




<h3>During Administration</h3>


<ul>


<li>Use proper technique.</li>

<li>Follow infection control procedures.</li>

<li>Observe the patient for reactions.</li>

<li>Never leave medications unattended.</li>


</ul>




<h3>After Administration</h3>


<ul>


<li>Document immediately.</li>

<li>Monitor patient response.</li>

<li>Report unusual reactions.</li>


</ul>


</div>







<h2>Medication Routes</h2>


<div class="card">


<p>

The route of administration determines how a medication enters the body.

</p>



<table>


<tr>

<th>Route</th>

<th>Meaning</th>

<th>Example</th>

</tr>



<tr>

<td>PO</td>

<td>By mouth (oral)</td>

<td>Tablets or liquids</td>

</tr>



<tr>

<td>SL</td>

<td>Sublingual (under tongue)</td>

<td>Rapid absorption medications</td>

</tr>



<tr>

<td>Buccal</td>

<td>Between cheek and gum</td>

<td>Medication placed in cheek</td>

</tr>



<tr>

<td>Topical</td>

<td>Applied to skin</td>

<td>Creams and ointments</td>

</tr>



<tr>

<td>Transdermal</td>

<td>Absorbed through skin</td>

<td>Medication patches</td>

</tr>



<tr>

<td>Ophthalmic</td>

<td>Eye administration</td>

<td>Eye drops</td>

</tr>



<tr>

<td>Otic</td>

<td>Ear administration</td>

<td>Ear drops</td>

</tr>



<tr>

<td>Nasal</td>

<td>Through the nose</td>

<td>Nasal sprays</td>

</tr>



<tr>

<td>IM</td>

<td>Intramuscular</td>

<td>Into muscle tissue</td>

</tr>



<tr>

<td>SubQ</td>

<td>Subcutaneous</td>

<td>Into fatty tissue</td>

</tr>



<tr>

<td>ID</td>

<td>Intradermal</td>

<td>Into the dermis layer</td>

</tr>


</table>


</div>







