<h2>💉 Injection Administration</h2>


<div class="card">


<p>

Injections require proper technique, infection control, and knowledge of the correct injection site and angle.

</p>


<h3>Parts of a Syringe</h3>


<ul>

<li><strong>Barrel:</strong> Holds the medication.</li>

<li><strong>Plunger:</strong> Pushes medication through the syringe.</li>

<li><strong>Tip:</strong> Connects to the needle.</li>

<li><strong>Needle:</strong> Used to deliver medication into tissue.</li>

</ul>


<h3>Parts of a Needle</h3>


<ul>

<li><strong>Hub:</strong> Connects the needle to the syringe.</li>

<li><strong>Shaft:</strong> Long metal portion of the needle.</li>

<li><strong>Bevel:</strong> Slanted tip that allows easier penetration.</li>

</ul>


</div>







<h2>Injection Types and Angles</h2>


<div class="card">


<table>


<tr>

<th>Injection Type</th>

<th>Location</th>

<th>Angle</th>

<th>Common Uses</th>

</tr>



<tr>

<td>Intradermal (ID)</td>

<td>Dermis layer of skin</td>

<td>5°–15°</td>

<td>TB testing, allergy testing</td>

</tr>



<tr>

<td>Subcutaneous (SubQ)</td>

<td>Fatty tissue under skin</td>

<td>45° or 90°</td>

<td>Insulin, some vaccines</td>

</tr>



<tr>

<td>Intramuscular (IM)</td>

<td>Muscle tissue</td>

<td>90°</td>

<td>Vaccines and medications</td>

</tr>


</table>


</div>







<h2>Common Injection Sites</h2>


<div class="card">


<h3>Intradermal (ID)</h3>

<ul>

<li>Inner forearm is commonly used.</li>

<li>Creates a small raised area called a wheal.</li>

<li>Used for diagnostic testing.</li>

</ul>




<h3>Subcutaneous (SubQ)</h3>

<ul>

<li>Abdomen</li>

<li>Back of upper arm</li>

<li>Anterior thigh</li>

</ul>




<h3>Intramuscular (IM)</h3>

<ul>

<li>Deltoid muscle</li>

<li>Vastus lateralis</li>

<li>Ventrogluteal site</li>

</ul>


</div>







<h2>Aseptic Technique</h2>


<div class="card">


<p>

Aseptic technique prevents contamination and reduces the risk of infection.

</p>


<ul>

<li>Wash hands before and after procedures.</li>

<li>Use sterile equipment.</li>

<li>Do not touch sterile parts.</li>

<li>Clean the injection site properly.</li>

<li>Dispose of sharps immediately.</li>

</ul>


</div>







<h2>Needle Safety and Sharps Disposal</h2>


<div class="card">


<ul>

<li>Never recap a used needle unless specifically required by safety procedures.</li>

<li>Dispose of needles immediately in a sharps container.</li>

<li>Do not overfill sharps containers.</li>

<li>Report needlestick injuries immediately.</li>

</ul>


</div>







<h2>Medication Documentation</h2>


<div class="card">


<p>

Documentation provides a legal record showing that medication was administered safely.

</p>



<h3>Document:</h3>


<ul>

<li>Patient name</li>

<li>Medication name</li>

<li>Dose</li>

<li>Route</li>

<li>Date and time</li>

<li>Injection site (if applicable)</li>

<li>Patient response</li>

<li>Name or initials of person administering medication</li>

</ul>


</div>







<h2>Medication Errors</h2>


<div class="card">


<p>

A medication error is any preventable event that may lead to incorrect medication use or patient harm.

</p>



<h3>Examples of Medication Errors</h3>


<ul>

<li>Wrong patient</li>

<li>Wrong medication</li>

<li>Wrong dose</li>

<li>Wrong route</li>

<li>Wrong time</li>

<li>Failure to document</li>

</ul>



<h3>If an Error Occurs:</h3>


<ul>

<li>Notify the provider immediately.</li>

<li>Assess the patient's condition.</li>

<li>Follow office policy.</li>

<li>Document the event accurately.</li>

<li>Complete an incident report if required.</li>

</ul>


</div>







<h2>Medication Storage</h2>


<div class="card">


<p>

Proper storage helps maintain medication effectiveness and safety.

</p>


<ul>

<li>Store medications according to manufacturer instructions.</li>

<li>Keep medications away from unauthorized individuals.</li>

<li>Check expiration dates regularly.</li>

<li>Store refrigerated medications at the correct temperature.</li>

<li>Protect medications from heat, moisture, and light when required.</li>

</ul>


</div>







<h2>Patient Education</h2>


<div class="card">


<p>

Medical assistants may help educate patients about safe medication use.

</p>


<h3>Teach Patients To:</h3>


<ul>

<li>Take medications exactly as directed.</li>

<li>Do not share medications with others.</li>

<li>Ask questions about unfamiliar medications.</li>

<li>Report unusual reactions.</li>

<li>Keep medications stored safely.</li>

</ul>


</div>







<h2>⭐ Final Exam Review</h2>


<div class="card">


<ul>

<li>Know the rights of medication administration.</li>

<li>Know medication routes and abbreviations.</li>

<li>Memorize injection angles: ID 5°–15°, SubQ 45°/90°, IM 90°.</li>

<li>Understand proper documentation.</li>

<li>Know medication safety procedures.</li>

<li>Always verify patient identity and allergies.</li>

</ul>


</div>



